      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's really strong. it definitely feels like he's making a structural argument. \n\ni want to push his character even further to really drive home the stakes for the reader. let's say he's talking to a group of young people who are skeptical and think the old ways are outdated. how would he respond to the idea that \"international cooperation is the only way to solve global problems like climate change or pandemics\"? \n\ni don't want him to just call them naive, but rather explain why he thinks those international institutions are actually the *cause* of the instability, rather than the solution. can you write a section where he dismantles the idea of \"global solutions\" and replaces it with his vision of how national strength is the real way to protect people?",
